orporate wall street executives  these ultra wealthy billionaires  they make huge investments in these  think tanks and advocacy organizations  and media outlets  that have been instrumental in  preserving defending and expanding these  dynamics such as what we see in the  criminal justice system such as what we  see in education and immigration  and and like you say they are profoundly  profitable these dynamics for the ultra  wealthy  um they make money off of mass  incarceration and immigrant detention  and immigration enforcement and school  privatization  and they also you know they pay less in  taxes when we as a society choose to  dehumanize  people rather than meeting their basic  human needs um  and then you know one thing that became  very clear i think to a lot of folks  last summer is that  systemic racism particularly you know  the mass criminalization and  incarceration  system that we have um it's extremely  effective  at controlling us at dividing us and  causing us not to recognize our common  interests  but we really saw that in how police  were used to  um to really stamp down um political  protest  so all those dynamics really help um  maintain the status quo help the ultra  wealthy maintain their political and  economic power  um and those are just you know i think  some of the ways that they profit from  these dynamics  yeah so and and that i mean that gives  us a good introduction to  a lot of different industries and  corporations that are sort of  directly connected to a lot of these  issues i'd be curious about your  thoughts before we go back to that  about i don't know i don't want to use  the term more like benign or pedestrian  corporations but just things more  associated with day-to-day like for  example  starbucks and other companies that last  summer started to make a big show  of we're going to do implicit bias  training and  all of our managers and maybe even  employees  are going to go through a course and so  on and so forth i've always  felt that that was essentially a pr  decision and and number one at  its face it didn't really pass the sniff  test for me but really what got me  thinking that  was i've not really found evidence that  that type of training  for a company like starbucks just to  pick one or others  really gets at the root of some of what  is causing these issues to begin with so  i know that we're this is kind of a  slight deviation from what you  you introduced to us but what's your  thought about that as as a source of  profit or at least of positive pr  yeah i mean you know you you make an  excellent point which is  you know last summer we had what seemed  like virtually every corporation in  america coming out with a statement  denouncing systemic racism  yes um you know proclaiming that black  lives matter  um and yet here we are you know  nine months later and what have those  corporations done  to actually dismantle these systems  right to  not just like you say do um  these sort of superficial diversity  efforts  or to you know donate some small  tiny percentage of their profits to  black owned businesses i'm talking about  you know real efforts to to address  these issues that are  harming and even killing um people in  black and brown communities  they're nowhere to be found and in fact  um for the most part there's there  they continue to be on the opposite side  of these fights so  you know one thing that um that i talk a  lot about  is is you know those  we've had those types of efforts forever  you know where  we we talk about how we're making  progress right we're taking steps  forward  well we've been doing that for over a  hundred years you know what i mean  and i it's got to be clear by now that  you know those things aren't bad per se  but they're not even close to being  enough  um and you know  i think the question for us is how many  more people are we going to allow to be  harmed  how many more lives are we going to  allow to be taken while we are content  not to actually solve the problem but to  merely make  progress so i think when it comes to  racial justice we need to remove the  word progress from our vocabulary  you know i think it's time to advance  real solutions  but to do so i think it's going to  require many more people understanding  how and why  corporate america and wall street are  able to profit from other people's pain  and we're going to need many more white  people in particular to understand how  their struggles are deeply  interconnected with the struggles of  people of color  so that they recognize that the the  fight for racial justice is also their  fight  now when you talk about that  interconnection i i'm i'm leading here  in the sense that i don't think you're  talking about a sort of class  reductionism of just realizing that  in the same ways that a lot of these  racial issues relate to class  that can also be a factor for you're  talking about a different type of  interconnection am i right  i am yeah i mean i think there's there's  there's different types of  interconnection but but one was really  only  made apparent to me when i was  researching and writing the book  um which is you know as i was diving in  so the book is really an effort to sort  of follow the money behind  systemic racism what i call strategic  racism to understand  exactly where it's coming from um and  and the ideology behind it um but as i  was doing that  um it was really eye-opening for me  because  you know as i was learning more about  where this money was coming and how  strategic these investments were i also  realized how heavily invested the ultra  wealthy are in pushing a political  agenda that's been deeply harmful  to white americans as well not in the  same ways of course and not always at  the same levels  frequently not but um but what i say is  that like  if white people examine the reasons that  their lives are far more difficult than  they need to be  they will likely eventually run into the  same set of organizations and  individuals who are leading the  opposition  against racial equality yeah so you know  i for one was astonished to discover  just how enormous an influence the ultra  wealthy have on my life  and on every other white person i know  but then yes beyond that like we all  need to get to the point where we can  clearly see that  education inequities and mass  criminalization and anti-immigrant  policies and all these other racial  justice issues  don't just harm those who attend the  under-resourced schools or suffer the  effects of what we're policing or  or face the prospect of being being  deported you know if we examine our  lives carefully  we will all find evidence of harm caused  by the reverberations  from these unjust systems um and on the  flip side we also need to recognize  that you know people who are fighting  for racial justice aren't trying to  like we're often treated like we're  trying to like ruin the world  but the reality is we're trying to build  a better world that benefits all people  and that when we do address these issues  of systemic racism  it doesn't just help people of color who  have the burden  of those racial inequities lifted off  them we all stand to benefit  when our neighbors are able to live  higher quality lives  and the really rot of injustice is  purged from our systems  what are some examples of where you  think the most notable progress has been  made like in the last 10 to 15 years on  these issues  well i you know i think the most um  exciting progress  um uh happening within um  most recent memory is is around law  enforcement and the criminal justice  system  and people are really starting to  interrogate  this issue of police and the role that  they  serve and the role that they should  serve right and people are asking  questions  really important questions about how we  actually keep  our um our communities safe  and healthy and why have we tasked  so much the police departments with so  much  that really they're they're ill-equipped  to handle i mean you know that that's  certainly true  in in black and brown communities but  but it's true beyond that too i mean i  think we have to recognize  how frankly how deeply we've lied  to communities of color over the year i  mean for 40 years basically  what u.s policymakers have been saying  to black and brown communities  is is this we can we can best keep you  safe by being tough on crime  by flooding your communities with police  officers by using stop and frisk by  employing  broken windows policing and cracking  down on low level offenses by putting  police in schools  by locking up people at the highest  rates anywhere in the world  by doing the type of policing frankly  that killed george floyd and dante  wright and so many others  and all along the consistent message has  been that these were the most effective  strategies for improving public safety  but there is no evidence for that you  know there are no heavily policed and  high incarceration communities that are  flourishing  socially economically culturally they're  just none  it's a null set um so it really is  um a deep deep lie um that that we've  been telling those communities for many  years and folks are starting to see  through it  and i think that's very exciting when  you think about um some of the decisions  that are made  by corporations that are not directly  involved for example like an  incarceration as you talked about  um or some some of these other specific  areas  um it seems that there is a kind of  vicious cycle  where even you know uh a chain  restaurant  based on how it decides where to put new  restaurants  and as a result where jobs are created  and what impact that has  on the food choices of people that may  otherwise live in food deserts i mean a  lot of these things seem very complex to  me because one can find  reasons to think that almost any deci  to give a really specific example think  of a fast food  chain that says hey you know what we do  well  in poor neighborhoods that are relative  food deserts in terms of other options  there's not that many grocery stores or  healthier  health healthier alternatives and so  that's where we're  going to go and this worsens the health  of the people in the neighborhood by  making okay  the counter though is hey they're  creating some jobs in that neighborhood  though if  every such business decided we're not  going to set up shop in this  neighborhood  that's a hundred that's 200 however many  jobs that that aren't brought there  and it very quickly becomes sort of like  cloudy how should what is the what  should be the top  level interpretation of some of these  business decisions that relate to race  how do we disaggregate that and start to  pick apart something  something like that  yeah i mean i think um  i think it's certainly true that we have  to become  much more disciplined as a country  in um analyzing all of those decisions  and the policies that influence those  decisions through the lens of race  through the lens of  systemic racism and i would suggest  strategic racism too  um because and i'll go back to  to criminal justice because we were  talking about it um  you know the last summer when george  floyd was killed  when he was murdered um we were told  over and over again that it was a  wake-up call  for the nation right um but we never  actually woke up  right um if we had we would have started  to have  we would have come together around real  solutions to those issues around the  vastly oversized and violent  criminal justice system that's used  within black and white communities as a  catch-all solution for  a huge variety of behaviors we didn't  actually do that we didn't even really  start that conversation  but it's not because we don't know how  it's not because they don't have a  fix like we do um and there is  um we don't do it because there's a very  well-funded opposition that stands in  the way  right so you have organizations like  alec  representing hundreds of the largest  corporations in the u.s working for  decades to pass those various  tough on crime policies you have folks  like the koch brothers  and all these other billionaires who've  been pouring tens of  billions of dollars into various  right-wing think tanks and advocacy  organizations that have been pushing  this agenda and still do today  you have one of those think tanks the  heritage foundation  who after the uprising last summer  against systemic racism  what they decided to do was launch a  back the blue campaign a back to blue  police pledge that has now been signed  by 200 members of congress  so that's 200 members of congress who  have vowed to continue supporting the  status quo in law enforcement  right and then one of those  is that we implement these public  policies that we know  are going to inflict needless harm on  large groups of people that we know will  perpetuate racial inequities  that we know will lead to people getting  killed and then when that harm becomes  apparent  we fail to address it appropriately and  significant part because it's  economically beneficial  for the ultra wealthy so i think that's  true in criminal justice i think that's  true in education i think that's true in  in public health um and  so what we really need to do is um if  we're going to address those issues we  really need to prioritize that  and lift up those issues and  collectively start looking at these  issues through the lens of race  so that we can start to design our  public policies  in a way that moves us towards the the  just and equitable society that  that all people deserve uh the book  is rich thanks to racism how the ultra  wealthy  profit from racial injustice we've been  speaking with the book's author  jim freeman jim so great having you on  and i really appreciate your time today  thank you so much for having me david

